WEBVTT

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:11.933
Babylon, over 2,600 years ago. King Nabopolassar's workmen cleared what was left of its temple-tower, Etemenanki, down to its foundation.

00:00:13.533 --> 00:00:20.445
For Babylon's people, a god's house belonged where the gods had set it: on its old foundation, to its old measures.

00:00:22.045 --> 00:00:27.640
A mistake would be laid into millions of bricks, by hand, and couldn't be moved once the tower rose.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:35.194
So before the first brick, they needed to know exactly where the old tower had stood, and how big it was.

00:00:36.994 --> 00:00:41.370
Well-trained specialists inspected the old foundation, and measured the site.

00:00:42.970 --> 00:00:51.308
Nor was it designed from nothing. A stepped tower of brick, with a temple on top, had been built in this land for over a thousand years.

00:00:52.908 --> 00:00:57.687
At Ur, in the south, part of one built more than four thousand years ago still stands.

00:00:59.287 --> 00:01:05.376
And before anyone built, the king asked the gods, through divination, to confirm the place and its measures.

00:01:06.976 --> 00:01:13.815
Only then did he set its foundation, in his words, "on its original socle": the base it had always stood on.

00:01:15.615 --> 00:01:22.186
The new tower rose on the exact spot of the old one, to the same measures, about ninety metres a side.

00:01:23.786 --> 00:01:27.442
Babylon's god had his house where his people believed it belonged.

00:01:29.042 --> 00:01:36.951
At the utility today, a new service can start the same way: from what its industry already knows, and what the utility already has.

00:01:38.351 --> 00:01:41.858
And nothing is built until the people who decide confirm it.

00:01:49.658 --> 00:01:56.990
The regional government, which owns the utility, asks for a plan: community batteries in three suburbs, in twelve weeks.

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:03.786
So many roofs there have solar panels that at noon, the streets can't take all their power.

00:02:05.386 --> 00:02:13.466
The voltage climbs, and the panels turn themselves down: on sunny days, 2,400 homes lose power they could have used.

00:02:15.266 --> 00:02:18.840
Farah, the customer advocate, hears it from them every summer.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:28.364
A community battery is one large battery on the street, shared by the homes around it: it stores their noon power for the evening.

00:02:29.964 --> 00:02:38.367
A new service needs a design: what the utility must be able to do, the steps from sign-up to credit, who does each, and what each must know.

00:02:40.167 --> 00:02:48.508
The last new service, a night tariff for charging cars, began with workshops that asked every team: what do you do, and how do you work?

00:02:50.108 --> 00:02:57.611
Asked to describe everything from nothing, the workshops ran for eight months, and households with electric cars waited a year.

00:02:59.411 --> 00:03:05.004
Twelve weeks leaves no room for that. People need something to react to, not a blank page.

00:03:08.304 --> 00:03:11.847
This time, Tomás asks the map agent for a first draft.

00:03:13.147 --> 00:03:22.049
It reads the industry's reference models: CIM, the IEC's list of what a distribution utility does, and Europe's market role model.

00:03:23.649 --> 00:03:31.324
And the utility's own documents: the government's request, the network's procedures, retail's product rules, and the map itself.

00:03:32.924 --> 00:03:41.702
In two days, it drafts the design: the capabilities, a value stream from sign-up to credit, the processes, and the information each uses.

00:03:43.502 --> 00:03:47.561
Fifty-eight elements, each one a draft until its owner confirms it.

00:03:49.161 --> 00:03:57.161
Reacting to a draft is faster than describing everything from nothing, and people answer a proposal better than an open question.

00:03:58.761 --> 00:04:00.629
So the workshops start from the draft.

00:04:03.729 --> 00:04:05.647
Every element says where it came from.

00:04:06.947 --> 00:04:14.647
Running the battery comes under operating the network, from the IEC's list; the battery, as information, from CIM.

00:04:16.247 --> 00:04:19.253
Crediting each bill comes from retail's product rules.

00:04:20.653 --> 00:04:27.845
Where no source says it, the draft says so: who decides when the battery charges is the agent's own guess, marked as a guess.

00:04:29.645 --> 00:04:37.019
And every element names who must confirm it: Grace for the network's, the head of retail for retail's, Ama for the rules.

00:04:38.619 --> 00:04:42.013
So each owner can check every line against what it came from.

00:04:45.113 --> 00:04:50.155
A tidy draft has a risk: people tend to nod through anything that looks confident.

00:04:51.855 --> 00:04:57.198
So Tomás doesn't ask the owners whether it's right. He asks what's missing, and what's wrong.

00:04:58.798 --> 00:05:06.212
Ama finds what's wrong. To invite households, the draft has retail use the network's list of homes with solar panels.

00:05:07.812 --> 00:05:15.392
That list is the network's. Customer information stays where it was collected, and the network must treat every retailer the same.

00:05:17.192 --> 00:05:21.289
Each source was right on its own. The step that joined them crossed the wall.

00:05:23.089 --> 00:05:30.245
So the network offers the battery to every retailer on the same terms, and each retailer invites its own customers.

00:05:31.845 --> 00:05:38.520
Grace finds what's missing. Before a crew works on a line, every source that could feed it is switched off and locked.

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:44.355
A battery on the street is a new source, and the draft never switches it off.

00:05:45.955 --> 00:05:52.948
No document said so: the network's procedures were written before batteries stood on streets. Grace adds the step.

00:05:56.248 --> 00:06:01.775
Each owner confirms their part, and each element they confirm carries their name and the date.

00:06:03.475 --> 00:06:05.860
The agent drafts. It never confirms.

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:13.139
What an owner corrects, it drafts again, for them to confirm. What they strike out leaves the map.

00:06:14.739 --> 00:06:22.481
In five weeks, the design is confirmed: 41 elements as drafted, 9 corrected, 8 struck out, and 4 added.

00:06:24.281 --> 00:06:27.182
Only what the owners confirmed becomes the model.

00:06:28.582 --> 00:06:31.270
The plan reaches the government in week eleven.

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:42.308
In Babylon, the builders started from a form a thousand years old and the foundation in the ground, and built once the place was confirmed.

00:06:44.008 --> 00:06:49.061
Nabopolassar called himself "the one who examines the old foundation of Babylon".

00:06:50.661 --> 00:06:58.684
At the utility, the plan started from the industry's models and its own documents, and only what the owners confirmed became the model.

00:07:00.284 --> 00:07:03.204
Five weeks, where the last new service took eight months.

00:07:04.604 --> 00:07:08.452
Next summer, the first battery's homes start storing their noon power.

00:07:09.852 --> 00:07:13.146
And not one household's details crossed the wall to get there.

00:07:18.846 --> 00:07:24.949
Start from a first draft, drawn from your industry's models and your own documents, with every source shown.

00:07:26.349 --> 00:07:30.511
Ask what's missing and what's wrong, and keep only what the owners confirm.

00:07:32.411 --> 00:07:34.083
Next: every change, checked.
